#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111625} :activity/id #uuid "d36a2bc5-aa3c-5982-9371-f19f06151a7c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048935 :topic/id #uuid "cd3dd2eb-8ac1-5d31-99ae-b58e078ea9ed" :topic/math-academy-id 1471} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111868 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{x}\\cos{x}-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\sin{x} = 0, \\qquad 0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x\\cos x - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin x &= 0 \\\\\n\\sin x(\\cos x - \\frac{1}{2}) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product rule, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0$. This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad \\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sin{x}=0$. The solutions to this equation that lie in the given domain are $x = 0^\\circ$ and $x=180^\\circ$.\n- Then, we solve $\\cos{x}-\\dfrac12=0$, or equivalently, $\\cos{x}=\\dfrac12$. The only solution to this equation that lies in the given domain is $x = 60^\\circ$.\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions of the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = 0^\\circ,\\quad x_2 = 60^\\circ,\\quad x_3 = 180^\\circ\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f48575a8-2964-5b25-9b20-92588072e29d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2575 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "da9cf3f1-fc61-57da-8b19-93d0f6af8437" :step/math-academy-id 447704 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110682 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110462 :question/id #uuid "4c5e27aa-f9a5-54c0-a15c-14b923eec893" :question/math-academy-id "e-3548"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "11c93af6-aa99-5071-8946-885cda9cd8e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050497}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111009}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "3fe85455-06f1-559a-b070-4fda1d70cc03" :step/math-academy-id 447705 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111627}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110683 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110461 :question/id #uuid "ef4a57fd-8ade-5b7d-9d6e-0fabd228e603" :question/math-academy-id "e-3546"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8553da6-42ce-5b77-97c4-7d9e2ee55f85" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111010}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "39f2a3f4-7276-5293-860f-6e6616556adc" :step/math-academy-id 447708 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111869 :tutorial/content "We can solve some trigonometric equations containing reciprocal functions using the zero product rule.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n(\\sec x -1)(2\\sin x +1)= 0, \\qquad -\\pi \\leq x < \\pi\n$$\n\nFirst, note that the function $\\sec{x}$ is not defined at the points where $\\cos{x}=0$. On the interval $[-\\pi,\\pi)$, the excluded values are\n\n$$\nx=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nThe equation is already factored, so by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} -1 =0, \\qquad 2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve $\\sec{x} -1 =0$, or equivalently, $\\sec{x} =1$. The only solution to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ is $x = 0$.\n- Then, we solve $2\\sin{x} + 1 = 0$, or equivalently, $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac12$. The solutions to this equation for $-\\pi \\leq x < \\pi$ are $x=-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$.\n\nCombining all the solutions from both equations, the solutions to the original equation (in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},\\quad x_2 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},\\quad x_3 = 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e82f262f-fd25-5e1a-90d3-7bdfe8e09095" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2576 :tutorial/title "Equations Containing Reciprocal Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ad98517c-7c49-5633-9f3e-c8a05d2183ff" :step/math-academy-id 447711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111629}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110684 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110463 :question/id #uuid "362274e9-6980-5e3b-896b-db42cfc41a9e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3550"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0f24e0-da03-5fa3-b3dc-bb383068ae15" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050313}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111147}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Containing Reciprocal Functions by Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "1f25f832-b48a-569a-9d0c-eaa3b450bf16" :step/math-academy-id 447712 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111630}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110685 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110464 :question/id #uuid "0aff48c8-380d-504b-a044-8ba08a2617a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3551"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9dea3f3b-5c9b-5005-996a-792a6902fcce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111180}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Some Coordinates on a Graph by Solving a Trigonometric Equation"} :step/id #uuid "00f0cdfe-17ea-519e-94fc-30cb570c2406" :step/math-academy-id 447715}] :activity/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110840}]}
